---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/company.css';
---
<BaseLayout title="FAQ" description="Straight answers about Chudware: browser versus native access, supported platforms, which files it opens and exports, migrating from another CAD system, where files live, how the AI copilot works, and commercial-preview status.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Frequently asked</p>
      <h1>Questions people actually ask first.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Answered from the product and the documentation. Where the honest answer is that something is not finalized, that is the answer given.</p>
      <ul class="co-faq-jump">
        <li><a href="#co-product">The product</a></li>
        <li><a href="#co-access">Access and platforms</a></li>
        <li><a href="#co-files">Files and data</a></li>
        <li><a href="#co-ai">AI copilot</a></li>
        <li><a href="#co-commercial">Commercial and support</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="co-product">
    <div class="co-faq-group wrap">
      <h2 id="co-product" class="co-anchor">The product</h2>
      <p>What Chudware is, and what it is not.</p>
      <div class="co-faq-list">
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>What is Chudware?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>A parametric CAD system for mechanical engineers, with an industrial solid-geometry kernel underneath it. One workspace covers constraint sketching, feature-based modeling, assemblies, CAM, <a href="/product/simulation/">simulation</a>, reverse engineering, PLM, and an AI copilot.</p>
            <p>It is a modeling system, not a viewer. Features stay ordered and editable, sketches can be fully defined, and the geometry is real B-rep rather than a mesh approximation.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Is this a web viewer with a CAD skin?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>No. The full application runs in your tab — the same product surface as the desktop build, not a stripped-down web edition and not a video stream of a machine in a data centre. Model state and tool calls travel over the wire to the engine service. Nothing about the toolchain is reduced to fit a web page.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>What can it simulate?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Linear static stress, modal (free-vibration) frequencies, and steady-state or transient thermal conduction, run against the part in the open document rather than in a separate application. High-cycle fatigue life is derived from the static stress field.</p>
            <p>Meshing is automatic, error-driven adaptive refinement is available, and a second, independent mesher can be switched in to check whether a result is a property of the part or of the mesh. What is not there is nonlinear or explicit-dynamics structural analysis on your actual geometry. The <a href="/product/simulation/">simulation page</a> sets out the scope and the caveats.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>How mature is it?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Chudware is in commercial preview. The product modes are real and used daily; distribution, packaging, and the public rate card are not finished. Shipped changes are listed in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, and the <a href="/gallery/">gallery</a> shows captures from the running product rather than concept renders.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="co-access">
    <div class="co-faq-group wrap">
      <h2 id="co-access" class="co-anchor">Access and platforms</h2>
      <p>Browser versus native, and how to get in.</p>
      <div class="co-faq-list">
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Browser or native desktop — which should I use?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Start in the browser if you want zero setup: it requires no installation and gives the full product surface. Choose native if you want the application running on your own workstation; signed builds for Windows, Linux, and macOS are issued to your account from the <a href="/download/">download page</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>What does the browser client actually run?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>The real Chudware application, running in your tab — the same product surface as the desktop build, with your keyboard and pointer driving the same tools directly. It is not a video stream of a machine in a data centre and it is not a viewer: model state and tool calls travel over the wire, so the geometry kernel and the full engineering toolchain stay intact. Your machine only needs a current desktop browser and a usable network connection — no graphics driver work, no installer, no licence dongle.</p>
            <p>Identity, authorization, and per-account file isolation are enforced by the gateway in front of the shared engine service. See <a href="/security/">security</a> for what that means concretely.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Which platforms are supported?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Any modern desktop browser</strong> — the zero-install route, on every platform.</li>
              <li><strong>Windows, Linux, and macOS</strong> native builds, downloaded from your account. All are 64-bit and need a graphics driver capable of a modern hardware-accelerated 3D context; without one the application does not start.</li>
              <li><strong>On Apple silicon</strong> the published macOS build is the Intel build, which runs through Rosetta 2. A native Apple-silicon build is not published yet.</li>
              <li><strong>Phones and tablets are not supported.</strong> This site reads fine on a phone; the CAD workspace does not. It is built around a keyboard, a precise pointer, and a large viewport, and there is no touch-first interface for it. Treat Chudware as a desktop product.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>No minimum RAM, CPU, or disk figures are published, because none have been measured and ratified. Opening the browser workspace is the honest way to find out whether a given machine is comfortable.</p>
            <p>Do not install anything presented as an official Chudware release from a third-party source; the only official source is the download page in your account.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>How do I get access?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p><a href="https://app.chudware.com/signup">Create an account</a> and verify your email address. Verification activates the account on a 14-day trial and signs you in; no payment card is required to start. Some registrations wait on operator approval before they become active, in which case the account page tells you so and no action is needed from you.</p>
            <p>Native builds are then issued from the <a href="/download/">download page</a>, which lists every platform and package format.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="co-files">
    <div class="co-faq-group wrap">
      <h2 id="co-files" class="co-anchor">Files and data</h2>
      <p>Formats, interoperability, and where your work lives.</p>
      <div class="co-faq-list">
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>What file format does Chudware use?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p><code>.step+</code> is the native format. It keeps the feature tree, dimensions, materials, studies, and drawings — the editable design, not a flattened result.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Can Chudware open a file somebody else made?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. It opens STEP (<code>.step</code>, <code>.stp</code>), IGES (<code>.iges</code>, <code>.igs</code>), and BREP solids directly, and reads STL, OBJ, PLY, and glTF meshes, sewing them into a solid body on open. DXF is read for 2D profiles — lines, arcs, circles, and polylines; splines, ellipses, text, dimensions, and hatch are skipped rather than half-imported.</p>
            <p>Imported geometry arrives as solid bodies you can measure, section, mate, and build new features on. It does not arrive carrying somebody else's feature history, because a neutral format does not store one.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Can I get my geometry into other tools?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Export STEP, IGES, BREP, STL, OBJ, PLY, or glTF when another tool needs a handoff. A neutral export loses the feature history by definition, so keep the <code>.step+</code> document as the source of truth and treat the export as a deliverable.</p>
            <p>The native document is not a lock-in either: <code>.step+</code> is a container that stores a neutral STEP body next to the parametric tree, so any CAD system can open the geometry inside it even if it cannot read the tree.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>I use SolidWorks, Fusion, or Onshape. Can I move my models across?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Through neutral formats, which is the path that actually works. Every one of those tools exports STEP or IGES; Chudware opens it as solid geometry and you continue modeling with new features on top.</p>
            <p>What Chudware does not do is read a native <code>.sldprt</code>, <code>.f3d</code>, or Onshape document and reconstruct the original feature tree, and no one should promise you that. There is an experimental Windows-only path that drives an installed, licensed copy of SolidWorks to convert its own files, but it is not something to plan a migration around.</p>
            <p>In practice, migration means bringing geometry over and rebuilding the parametric intent where you need it to be editable — which is the same trade you make moving between any two CAD systems.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Where are my files stored?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>In a per-account file tree on the server, with a separate per-account upload bucket. Path resolution is clamped to your own roots on every request, including on the engine connection itself, so another signed-in account cannot address your documents by guessing a path.</p>
            <p>Storage is quota-checked per account. You can export your files at any time, and you should: Chudware publishes no recovery-time or data-loss objectives, so it should not be the only copy of a design you care about.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Who can see my designs?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Project content enters Chudware systems only through the hosted workspace, synchronization, or managed AI features you use. Chudware does not sell personal information and does not use project content to train a public model. The full statement is in the <a href="/legal/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="co-ai">
    <div class="co-faq-group wrap">
      <h2 id="co-ai" class="co-anchor">AI copilot</h2>
      <p>What it does inside the model, and whose API keys pay for it.</p>
      <div class="co-faq-list">
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>What does the copilot actually do?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>It operates the same modeling tools you do. Describe the engineering intent — the part, the critical dimensions, what must not change — and the copilot inspects geometry, creates or edits features, renders views, and measures results in the open document. The output is a normal model: ordinary sketches and features in the same feature tree, which you can inspect, edit, or delete.</p>
            <p>Details are in the <a href="/docs/ai-copilot/">copilot guide</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Whose API keys does it use?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Your choice, made during onboarding. Chudware-managed capacity consumes Chudware credits. If you add your own provider key instead, that work is sent through the provider you configured, is governed by that provider's terms and charges, and consumes no Chudware credits.</p>
            <p>Provider keys are secrets: paste them only into Chudware's AI setup screen, never into a chat message or a project note.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Can I rely on what the copilot produces?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Review it. AI output can be incomplete or wrong, and neither the copilot nor Chudware certifies a design, analysis, toolpath, or machine setup. Anything that drives physical equipment requires explicit operator review of stock, tool, setup, simulation, and posted code before it runs.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="co-commercial">
    <div class="co-faq-group wrap">
      <h2 id="co-commercial" class="co-anchor">Commercial and support</h2>
      <p>Pricing status, trials, and how to reach a person.</p>
      <div class="co-faq-list">
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>What does it cost?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Public rates are not published yet. Chudware is in commercial preview and the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> deliberately shows the ways the product can be used instead of invented figures. Rates, storage allowances, credit bundles, and tier boundaries appear once they are ratified.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Is there a trial?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Yes — 14 consecutive days, with no payment card required. Education pricing may be offered to eligible <code>.edu</code> accounts where it is available; exact terms are shown before any purchase.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Are AI credits refundable?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>No. Credit packs are non-refundable and non-transferable, are not cash or stored value, and may be used only with the purchasing account. See the <a href="/legal/terms/">terms of service</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>How do I report a security issue?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Email <a href="mailto:security@chudware.com?subject=Security%20report">security@chudware.com</a> with the affected surface, the impact, and reproduction steps, and test only against your own account. There is no bug bounty and no formal response-time commitment; reports reach the team that writes the code. The <a href="/security/">security page</a> sets out what is enforced and what is not claimed.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="co-faq">
          <summary>Something is broken. Who do I talk to?</summary>
          <div class="co-faq-answer">
            <p>Start with <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">troubleshooting</a>, then mail <a href="mailto:support@chudware.com">support@chudware.com</a>. Every other route — sales, privacy, legal — is listed on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="wrap section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Still unanswered</p>
      <h2>Ask the question this page missed.</h2>
      <p class="lede">The documentation covers day-to-day use; anything else can go to a person who will answer it directly.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/docs/">Read the docs</a><a class="button button-secondary" href="/contact/">Contact Chudware</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
